Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog** audit for your integration test suite.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · .NET 10
**Integration calibration:** Applied — class is annotated `[TestCategory("Integration")]`. Mystery Guest, Eager Test, and Magic Number severities were evaluated with integration norms. All 15 remaining smells from the catalog were checked and came back clean.

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ← blocks for 3 seconds unconditionally
```

Even in integration tests, a hard `Thread.Sleep` is a smell — it makes the suite slow and non-deterministic. If the event fires in 50ms the test still waits 3s; if the system is under load 3s may not be enough.

**Fix — use a polling/retry loop with a timeout:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100);
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
    while (sw.Elapsed < timeout)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(interval);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification was not sent within the timeout window.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures, slow suite, false negatives on overloaded agents.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

One branch will never execute, creating a hidden testing gap. The test gives the illusion of coverage for both paths but only ever runs one.

**Fix — write two explicit tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsName()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasDiscount()
{
    var user = new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m);
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com") as PremiumUser;

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Silent gap — the untaken branch is never tested, but coverage tools won't warn you.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion — passes even if every Insert silently fails
}
```

This test will pass as long as no exception is thrown — it verifies nothing about actual data persistence.

**Fix — assert the observable outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count,
        "Expected all 100 bulk-inserted users to be persisted.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — 100% pass rate with 0% behavioral verification.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions, zero failure messages. When one fails in CI you get `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<(null)>` — which tells you nothing about *why* or *which property* was under test.

**Fix — add message parameters:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be auto-assigned on insert.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage — developers must open source to identify which property failed.

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining **4 tests** are well-designed:

| Test | Notes |
|------|-------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused scope |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single concern, negative assertion confirms deletion |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count `3` is contextually obvious (3 inserts precede it) — not a Magic Number |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Good async/parallel stress test with proper `await` and assertion |

The **General Fixture** is also clean — both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test method, so the `[TestInitialize]` setup is appropriately scoped.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` after bulk insert | ~2 min |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** | Split into two focused test methods | ~5 min |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll-retry loop | ~10 min |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** | Add message strings to 4 assertions | ~2 min |

Start with **#1** — it's the highest-risk smell (false pass) and the fastest fix. The Sleepy Test fix takes longest but will pay dividends in CI speed and reliability.
